// The baseline pack: cross-project working discipline, the task lifecycle, and
// the general engineering skills. Declared explicitly like every other pack — no pack is active by
// default. Bootstrap's --init seeds the declaration and the nightly update
// backfills it into existing consumers; never fingerprinted (the declaration is
// authoritative — dropping it is a deliberate choice).
//
// Its skills/ holds general engineering practice every project's work can call for,
// whatever its technology, mounted wherever basics is declared (which --init seeds
// everywhere). When one stops being a baseline activity, its directory moves to the
// pack whose projects need it (#385 moved the git/GitHub and Claudinite-lifecycle
// skills out).
import { contributedBarrierRules } from './barriers.mjs';

export default {
  // A migration record's declared `version` must be ≤ this number, and this number must
  // MOVE for that record to reach a member already at the previous one:
  // `migrationApplies` is `want > have` against the stamped version, and what gets
  // stamped is this manifest's number — so a record declaring a version above it would
  // re-apply every cycle, forever, draining never.
  version: '60905.1',
  minEngineVersion: '60822.1',
  ruleRoutingGuidance: {
    belongs: 'cross-project working discipline, issue-branch-PR lifecycle, repo hygiene, doc/reference integrity and the baseline engineering, testing and debugging skills',
    excludes: 'technology-specific content — its own tech pack; git procedure and GitHub Actions workflow or platform behaviour — git-github',
  },
  seededByDefault: true,
  // `core` is required rather than assumed: this pack is declared everywhere, so
  // the closure is what puts Claudinite's own rules in front of every session.
  // git-github carries the git/GitHub side of the task lifecycle (#385).
  requires: ['claudinite-lifecycle', 'git-github'],
  // The directed folder-access graph, absorbed from the `barriers` pack (#1681):
  // the config-driven `barrier` rule in worldRules/, and this seam, which reads the
  // FIXED barriers other active packs carry as manifest data
  // (contributes: { barriers: [...] }) and builds each into a first-class rule.
  // Composition is declaration + configuration, never a code import
  // (pack-independence) — which is the property the mechanism exists to provide.
  // The guide is barriers.md; the contribution key keeps its own name because it
  // names the mechanism rather than the pack that used to house it.
  contributedRules: (activePacks) => contributedBarrierRules(activePacks),
  // Rules that audit the repo as it stands, whatever this session did.
  // warning-suppression and rules-line-length are declared checks in this
  // pack's declared-checks.json, discovered structurally beside these.
  // Rules that judge the change and the session in front of you — the branch's
  // commits, the diff, the conversation. task-lifecycle and squash-merge-history
  // are declared checks carrying scope: "work", discovered structurally beside
  // these.
  // `task-janitor` and `ci-performance` are this pack's scheduled tasks,
  // discovered by the scheduler's filesystem scan (packs/claudinite-tasks/discover.mjs)
  // rather than declared here.
};
---
# Version history

Records for `packs/basics/pack.mjs`'s `version` field, one row per bump — added going forward from
the version this file was introduced beside (60821.4); earlier bumps are not backfilled.

| Version | Date | What changed |
|---|---|---|
| 60905.1 | 2026-09-05 | Task declarations name what the run does to pull requests in the four-value `expected_outcome` vocabulary (#1695): `pr` became `fresh_pr` and `none` became `no_code_changes`, the same behaviour under the word that now sits beside `amend_existing_or_create_new_pr` and `supersede_existing_pr` — except that `ci-performance` declares `amend_existing_or_create_new_pr` (owner, 2026-09-04), its `task.md` delivering on the branch and pull request the item names. |
| 60904.2 | 2026-09-04 | The directed folder-access graph is absorbed from the retired `barriers` pack (#1681): the config-driven `barrier` rule, the pack-contribution seam and the guide ([barriers.md](barriers.md)) live here, a repo's graph moves to this entry's `config.barriers`, and the mechanism no longer asks an adoption question nobody chose to answer. |
| 60903.4 | 2026-09-03 | The legacy-tolerance rule and `writing-migration-plans` re-gate a tolerance's removal on a stated convergence window rather than on a condition that reads back true. A census — "when no holder still carries the old shape" — sounds like the rigorous choice and is usually unanswerable, so the removal waits on an answer that never arrives; the skill now says to read a count only where you own both sides of it, and the phase exit-condition example stops assuming another repo's state is observable from here (#1652). |
| 60903.3 | 2026-09-03 | A legacy tolerance is scaffolding, not a feature: `RULES.md` gains the rule that one ships with an advisory firing where the old shape is still in use and with its removal already a link in the migration's chain, and the migration bullet names that removal among the code written before approval. `writing-migration-plans` gains the section behind it — the three things that ship with a tolerance, the gate that reads back true rather than a date, making an unreadable gate the chain's first link, and the asymmetry that decides between a narrow policy and `nothing` — plus the review finding for a tolerance with neither advisory nor removal link (#1637). |
| 60903.1 | 2026-09-03 | A skill's `SKILL.md` opens on what to do, not on what the skill is: the self-describing framing and the pointers to prose the reader already holds are gone. |
| 60903.2 | 2026-09-02 | `RULES.md` drops the descriptive framing the pack README already carries — the file carries rules only. |
| 60902.5 | 2026-09-02 | `file-placement`'s pack-module pattern drops the retired `.claudinite/local_packs/` root: a repo's own packs are discovered from `.claudinite/local/packs/` and nowhere else (#1627). |
| 60902.4 | 2026-09-02 | Three rules on what human review is for: an automerge policy is a prediction of the change's folders and kinds, never widened to fit the diff; `nothing` for anything a later PR cannot undo; a diff wider than its ask is reported in the hand-over as a separation-of-concerns lesson. `writing-migration-plans` gains the sizing questions — how many PRs, each link's predicted diff and the policy it becomes, phases against divisions — and how the chain survives a policy park, a red CI and a PR closed unmerged (#1613). |
| 60902.3 | 2026-09-02 | `writing-handover-issues` gains the producer/consumer adjacency rule: a step that mints or reveals a value and the step that pastes it where it lives go immediately after each other, rather than a block of producers followed by a block of consumers. Every box between them is a value the reader holds in their head while doing something else, and one shown once is held there at the price of minting it again (#1592). |
| 60902.2 | 2026-09-02 | `ci-performance` converts to the declarative `preconditions`: `['substantive-change || prs-touched']`. It also narrows — any commit used to wake it, and another task's own delivery landing is the machinery running, not code whose CI timings could have moved (#1578). |
| 60902.1 | 2026-09-02 | The retired `merged-pr`/`open-pr` outcome spellings are swept out of the pack's own prose and fixtures that were not exercising the acceptance door: `writing-tests`' example assertion, and `ci-performance`'s task.md/task.mjs comments, which also correct their stale "always reviewed" landing description to the current `automerge: 'anything'` ceiling (#1470). |
| 60901.4 | 2026-09-01 | The pack adopts the references convention: `references.md` carries the incident evidence behind `verify-in-production` (#1121, #1460, the queue-park and label incidents), `do-later` (#1160), `writing-tests` (#429, #310, the hostname-apex case) and the `declared-check-spec-keys` check, and those prose files end the migrated rules with bare `(n)` markers instead of inline issue ids (#1564). |
| 60901.3 | 2026-09-01 | `authoring-agent-docs` narrows to the instruction files outside a pack: the "Shape a rule so it fires" house style moves to claudinite-growth's new `writing-pack-prose` skill, which also owns the per-pack `references.md` rationale convention, and a pointer says which skill to load when the file being written lives in a pack (#1560). |
| 60901.2 | 2026-09-01 | `declared-check-spec-keys` reads the engine's vocabulary through a namespace import and a capability guard: the pack lane delivers nightly and the engine lane only on a release, so this file spends a window beside an engine with no `unplacedSpecKeys` to export — and a named import of an absent export is a link-time error the loader records as a fault, which fails the self-test and parks the converge. On such an engine the rule is inert, which is the behaviour that engine already has (#1400). |
| 60901.1 | 2026-09-01 | `declared-check-spec-keys` — a declared check's key that the engine's vocabulary cannot place is now dropped at load and reported by this rule rather than thrown on, so a declaration written for a newer engine loads (with the unknown key inert) instead of dropping every rule in its file and wedging the member's converge. Advisory, because in a member the key is as likely to be skew as a typo; the canon overrides it to blocking, where engine and declarations ship from one commit (#1400). |
| 60831.6 | 2026-08-31 | `verify-in-production` gains the coded form (#1530): a URL-readable artifact files declarative `Live-probe:`/`Verify-probe:` lines routed to `claudinite-tasks/verify-production`, judged in code Action-side — no session, no egress wall, no `Model:`. The reachability gate becomes runner routing (URL → coded, GitHub read → agentic, neither → file nothing), and a fresh coded verification is forced to run at filing time and watched reporting **not yet live**, so the probes are proven failing before the release lands. |
| 60831.5 | 2026-08-31 | `writing-handover-issues` — an issue a PERSON executes is an instrument, not a description, so its body is a checklist and nothing else, with every explanation (what each step buys, what breaks while it is undone) below the last box rather than between them. Each box is one imperative line carrying the deepest URL that lands on the screen rather than a settings breadcrumb, and a step that produces a value names the control that mints it and where on that screen it appears. `verify-in-production`, `writing-migration-plans` and lifecycle's `adopt-pack` each re-spelled a fragment of that shape and now point here (#942). |
| 60831.4 | 2026-08-31 | The reachability gate is a filter rather than a router: an artifact the run cannot read is a reason to file nothing and say the change is unverified, and asking a person is a rare exception earned by a costly silent failure — not the fallback whenever no automatic check exists. A fleet-wide subject belongs to a routine holding fleet scope or to nobody. The same bar reaches every ad-hoc item in `RULES.md`: a marked issue asks an unattended session on this repo to do the work, and work it cannot do here parks minutes after being picked (#1520). |
| 60831.3 | 2026-08-31 | `production-retrospective` — an element that earned a design doc or a phased tracking issue schedules the review that comes back ~a week after it works as planned, asking working / misused / overused / underused against its production record. The brief is written at design time — expected amounts, behaviours, the signals for misuse, overuse and a wrong decision, the re-examinable decisions, and the metrics the run will read (built with the element) — never improvised at run time; findings route by kind, implementation faults to task issues and disproven decisions to discussion issues parked on the owner. Filed on the ad-hoc lane as a migration chain's last link or beside `verify-in-production` at the merge; the lane is open to further classes — canon-pack, local-pack or fleet (#1501). |
| 60831.2 | 2026-08-31 | `verify-in-production`'s playbook converges through `converge-item.mjs`, which needs no REST route and is the whole path on every session — and never a `task:status:*` label written by hand, which records a pass nowhere the queue reads and then hides the item from the leash (#1499). |
| 60831.1 | 2026-08-31 | `verify-in-production` gates on what the RUN can read before filing: a GitHub read in the routine's scope gets the automatic form, and a web page, deployed site, console or dashboard gets the human-step form — an ordinary assigned issue with no mark, rather than a queue item that spends a session rediscovering the wall and parks. The "watch it now" test asks whether the artifact becomes readable inside this session, not only whether it already did (#1500). |
| 60830.6 | 2026-08-30 | `do-later` proposes an `Automerge:` policy from the folder first — a deferral's tree is known at filing time, and a folder bound holds where a repo-wide kind class does not — reaching for a bare kind only where no single tree bounds the change, and for review where several unrelated trees do (#1483). |
| 60830.5 | 2026-08-30 | `runnable-doc-commands` — a `node <path>` written in a pack doc is a command an agent will run, and nothing opens it until one does; the check judges the two shapes the corpus owns (a placeholder-rooted path by its suffix, a mount path against the canon beneath it) and leaves a consuming repo's own paths alone (#1475). |
